{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Since the onset of Coronavirus Disease 2019 (COVID19), SARS-CoV-2 infection has been circulating globally, with approximately six peak waves of outbreaks worldwide before May 26, 2023, with a cumulative number of confirmed SARS-CoV-2 infections of 76,689,575 and a total number of deaths of 6,935,889 [1], posing a serious threat to the health of populations in all countries. ... The fact that SARS-CoV-2 is still mutating indicates that it remains a global health threat. The WHO study published in Nature [1, 3] also suggested that accurately tracking SARS-CoV-2 and its impact had been challenging.",
      "reason": "The introduction describes the global burden, ongoing mutation, and challenges/uncertainties in tracking SARS-CoV-2, establishing the current state of knowledge and important uncertainties."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "Background There is a significant increase in the number of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection reports in various countries. However, the trend of reinfection rate over time is not clear. ... Conclusion Meta-regression analysis found that the overall reinfection rate increased and then decreased over time, followed by a period of plateauing and then a trend of increasing and then decreasing, but the peak of the second wave of reinfection rate was lower than the first wave. SARS-CoV-2 is at risk of reinfection and the Omicron variant has a higher reinfection rate than other currently known variants. The results of this study could help guide public health measures and vaccination strategies in response to the Coronavirus Disease 2019 (COVID-19) pandemic.",
      "reason": "The abstract states the unclear trend in reinfection rates and explicitly explains that the study's results can guide public health measures and vaccination strategies, justifying the importance and need for the review."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_15",
      "quote": "The search strategy we used was based on previous studies, and the process of literature screening and information extraction strictly followed PRISMA standards. In addition, the advantage of this study was that compared with the previous meta-analysis of the SARSCoV-2 reinfection rate, first, to avoid the heterogeneous impact of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection definition on SARSCoV-2 reinfection rates, so this study defined reinfection and included only literature with reinfection intervals greater than 90 days.",
      "reason": "The discussion explicitly compares this study to previous meta-analyses and explains how the current review differs and why it was needed (e.g., definition harmonization and meta-regression for time-varying rates)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The review concerns reinfection rates (observational epidemiology) rather than intervention effects; no intervention mechanism is proposed in the evidence."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The review does not evaluate a complex intervention or delivery context and provides no logic model; this requirement does not apply."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 84
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_4",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e1",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "Therefore, the aim of this study was to conduct a meta-analysis to describe and summarize the current reinfection rates in different regions and among diverse populations. Furthermore, the study sought to examine trends in reinfection rates over time, so as to provide reference for the development of global unified reinfection standards and reinfection measures in the future.",
      "reason": "The objectives of the review are explicitly stated (to describe/summarize reinfection rates and examine time trends), but they are not presented using a recognised question‑formulation framework (e.g., PICO or similar)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e2",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "We searched PubMed, Web of Science, Medline, Embase, Cochrane Central Register of Controlled Trials, China National Knowledge Infrastructure, and Wanfang for cohort studies, case-control studies, and cross-sectional studies up to March 16, 2023, to conduct a meta-analysis of global SARS-CoV-2 reinfection rate.",
      "reason": "This review synthesises observational studies of reinfection rates rather than intervention effects, so specifying planned comparisons using PICO (an intervention-focused framework) does not apply."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 85
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_5",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "The inclusion criteria were: (1) Conformed to research questions, i.e., studies that estimated the reinfection rate of SARS-CoV-2 or we could calculate it based on the data provided in the paper; (2) The study design included cohort study, case-control study, and descriptive study; (3) The study fited our working definition of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection. ... reinfection working definition in this study was based on a positive laboratory result at least 90 days after laboratory confirmation of primary infection ... (4) Original research. The exclusion criteria were: (1) The sample size was less than 20; (2) Belonged to one of the following types of studies: editorial, case report, case series study, systematic review, meta-analysis, animal experiment, news report; (3) Secondary reporting or articles on repeated studies of the same population; (4) Grey literature, including a range of documents not controlled by commercial publishing organizations.",
      "reason": "The text explicitly lists study characteristics used to determine eligibility: eligible study designs (cohort, case-control, descriptive), a working reinfection definition (>=90 days), minimum sample size, original research requirement, and excluded study types and grey literature."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e2",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "To avoid language bias, the search was not limited to any language. We conducted preliminary screening based on the English title and English abstract provided by nonEnglish articles. Further, in the screening process, if the included article was neither in English nor in Chinese and did not provide an English abstract or full text, we used an online translator (Google Translate) for translation. ... The exclusion criteria were: ... (4) Grey literature, including a range of documents not controlled by commercial publishing organizations.",
      "reason": "The review specifies language handling (no language limit, use of translation) and excludes grey literature (report/publication status), but does not state any dissemination year restriction; thus some report-characteristic criteria are specified but not all (year is not addressed)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_8",
      "quote": \"One of them reported only the reinfection rate, but not the associated number of first infections as well as the number of reinfections, and two other articles studied the same population, so the meta-analysis of single-group rates could not be performed. Therefore, we excluded the above literature with the number of unreported infections and selected one of two literature with the same population for inclusion.\",
      "reason": "The authors explicitly state exclusion of studies that did not report necessary outcome numbers (e.g., number of first infections and reinfections), distinguishing exclusion due to unreported outcome data."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "table_0",
      "quote": \"Subgroup analyses were performed for age, reinfection variant, country, study population, and study type respectively ... Table 1 Results of subgroup analysis\"",
      "reason": "The review specifies groups used in syntheses (age groups, variants, country, study population, study type) and presents subgroup results and comparisons linked to review objectives."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e5",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_19",
      "quote": \"However, the limitations of this study are as follows: first, we did not include the 'grey literature' , which may lack potential relevant studies that meet the inclusion criteria [35]. ... In order to avoid data heterogeneity in metaanalysis while ensuring data quantity, we used previous literature and previous definitions of reinfection in some countries as the criteria for included literature and data extraction in this study.\",
      "reason": "The authors provide rationales for notable restrictions (excluding grey literature and using certain reinfection definition thresholds to limit heterogeneity and ensure sufficient data), explaining why these restrictions were applied."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "85"
}

